A||N||7||FieroJoe||84 SE - E-brake adjustment, calipers ain't workin'||||13||
Z||000000||FieroJoe||09-29-2001||12:27 AM||||I've been working on these parking brakes for an 84 SE that I'm fixing up for a guy for too long now.  First off, the car has been sitting for about 3 years, never started or moved really, and it has 60k on it.<P>Next, I have replaced the front and left cables, since they were completely seized up from never being used.  Now the problems start.<P>Got the cables in, put the wheels on and went to adjust the brakes at the equalizer, exactly as I had to do with my 87 coupe.  Eventually, the equalizer nut wouldn't go any further, and the e brakes wouldn't hold one bit.<P>So next, I took the cables and springs off the lever arms on the calipers, and pressed the lever by hand until it was bottomed out.  The brakes held, solid as a rock.  Thinking that maybe I broke something loose again, since the calipers were siezed up in their slides (no grease left, just dried crap), I put the cables and springs back on.  Tried it with the cable tensioned to the max at the equalizer, and nope, dosent hold at all.  Then, I looked at the lever arms on the calipers, and noticed that the compression of the spring still takes up enough space that the lever can't go down as far as I held it with my hand.<P>For those of you still with me, question:  Should the adjuster screw, that which pushes the piston out when the lever arm is pulled, automatically adjust for the wear of the pads?  The Helm manual says nothing about adjusting this mechanism for the e brake, just the cable equalizer.  What I'm thinking is that maybe something inside there with the screw has broken so the screw dosent start to turn until the lever arm is extended all the way.  I know that GM had a crap load of problems with this wierd design, and wondered if maybe just the whole caliper needs to be replaced.  And yes I should mention this, both sides are like this, so both calipers would need to be replaced.  I didn't have any of this trouble with my 87 coupe when I put in the same 2 cables (seized) and all new pads too.<P>So, any ideas?  Thanks for reading.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||13
Z||000001||theogre||09-29-2001||02:02 PM||||The pads probably have too much clearance. this is a very common issue with the rear brakes.<P>Before you assume the calipers are bad, run thru the manual adjustment procedure in my cave. And yes... the listed shim is required.<P>Once the calipers are properly adjusted the ebrake arms will need far less rotation to go tight.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||09-29-2001||04:41 PM||||Followed your instructions, made your shim, still dosent work.<P>When the shim is in there, and I am holding the piston & pad tight against the rotor, the lever arm moves about half as much as it did until the calipers lock.  Also, when I pryed the piston out, it didnt move much at all, the pad was already pretty tight against the rotor when the shim is in there.<P>So, I take the shim out, reinstall the spring, and step on the brakes many times.  I press the lever down by hand until it can't move any more, and the pads barely drag.  What now?||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||09-29-2001||10:04 PM||||bump||||reg||1
Z||000004||FieroMaster88||09-30-2001||01:17 AM||||&lt;Bump&gt;<P>Well..you could try hitting them a couple times...will at least make you feel better! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>If after hitting them they stil dont work...use a bigger hammer! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jamessig.jpg">              <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>Not your ordinary 88 coupe!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||09-30-2001||06:56 AM||||If I were you I wouldn't stop with just the left and right cables. It sounds like the front cable is WAY streched if the nut on the adjuster is bottomed out and still have no e-brake.<BR>Just a thought.<P>IRG||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||09-30-2001||01:15 PM||||could be the calipers a worn... the adjustment procedure is always worth trying.<P>Just to make sure,,, once the shim was in and you got the brakes tight, you pried out the piston and held it while you worked the ebrake lever?? And you are sure the shim is the right thickness, 0.010 inch. (The range is 010-015 but 010 is better.)<P>try putting the shim in and step on the pedal to seat it. then go theru the prying and stuff.<P>Also... make certain the caliper slides are free. If they bind you will have problems.||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroJoe||09-30-2001||06:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Indiana_resto_guy:<BR><B>If I were you I wouldn't stop with just the left and right cables. It sounds like the front cable is WAY streched if the nut on the adjuster is bottomed out and still have no e-brake.<BR>Just a thought.<P>IRG</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, the front cable has been replaced, brand new.  The nut is so tight because the calipers arent adjusted or are bad.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR> the adjustment procedure is always worth trying.<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes, I did follow your adjustment procedure 3 times for each caliper.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>And you are sure the shim is the right thickness, 0.010 inch. (The range is 010-015 but 010 is better.)<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Looks like I found my problem!  I must have read the thickness wrong by a decimal or something becuase I just measured it with a micrometer and mine's .045"  I guess that might be a problem  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR><BR>Also... make certain the caliper slides are free. If they bind you will have problems<BR><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>They sure are, I regreased all of them before I started working on the ebrakes.  I can slide the calipers by hand.<P>Now that I find that my shim is too big, I will have to try it again with the proper thickness shim.  I am going to talk to the owner tomorrow and ask him if he wants me to continue working on the car, becuase right now the bill is up to $1,200.||||reg||1
